[moddesc {NaviServer Built-in Commands}]

[titledesc {Seek in an open file for a string}]

[description]

Searches an open, readable, seekable file channel [arg channelId] for the
literal string [arg searchstring], beginning at the channel's current position.
The search is case-sensitive and does not interpret pattern metacharacters.

[para]
When found, the command returns the [emph {absolute byte offset}] of the first
matching byte and positions the channel there. Offsets are not character
indices, even when the file contains UTF-8 or other multibyte data. The next
read starts at the match. To find a later occurrence, advance the channel
explicitly before calling again; otherwise the same match is returned.

[para]
If no match is found, [const -1] is returned and the channel is positioned at
physical end-of-file. Read and seek failures raise an error rather than
returning [const -1]. Non-seekable channels are rejected before reading.

[para]
The search reads file bytes without newline conversion, character decoding or
recognition of a configured EOF character. The channel's encoding, newline
translation, EOF-character, buffer-size and blocking options are unchanged. This byte-offset behavior
also applies after Tcl [cmd read] or [cmd gets] has buffered input, and supports
binary file contents such as multipart upload bodies.

[para]
The search string uses Tcl's UTF-8 string representation, as in earlier versions
of this command. It must be nonempty and at most 32767 bytes in that
representation. The command does not convert the search string according to
the channel's encoding.

